The attached image (Figure 5 from the PDF) demonstrates how Nano-11 facilitates antigen transport to lymph nodes. Panel A shows flow cytometry plots indicating that Nano-11 formulations (OVA+N11) lead to a higher percentage and mean fluorescence intensity (MFI) of OVA-positive cells in draining lymph nodes compared to soluble OVA or OVA with aluminum hydroxide (OVA+AH) at day 1 and 2. Panels B and C quantify these findings, showing significantly increased OVA MFI per cell and a higher percentage of MigDCs among OVA-positive cells for Nano-11, indicating more efficient antigen delivery and specific targeting to these immunopotentiating cells compared to traditional adjuvants.

The attached image significantly contributes to cancer research by illustrating Nano-11's potential as an adjuvant for therapeutic cancer vaccines. Its ability to efficiently deliver tumor-associated antigens to migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) in draining lymph nodes is critical. This targeted delivery can enhance the activation of anti-tumor T cells, crucial for breaking immune tolerance and mounting effective adaptive immune responses against cancer, thereby improving the efficacy of immunotherapy strategies.